2026-11-07T06:33:15.5729877Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5730613Z 3. Create the system users [34m(uids [30001..30008])[0m and groups [34m(gid 30000)[0m
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5731416Z    that the Nix daemon uses to run builds. To create system users
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5731950Z    in a different range, exit and run this tool again with
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5732344Z    NIX_FIRST_BUILD_UID set.
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5732584Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5732872Z 4. Perform the basic installation of the Nix files daemon.
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5733212Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5733455Z 5. Configure your shell to import special Nix Profile files, so you
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5734004Z    can use Nix.
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5734220Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5734361Z 6. Start the Nix daemon.
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5734576Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5735104Z [32m[4;32mWould you like to see a more detailed list of what I will do?
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5735796Z [0mNo TTY, assuming you would say yes :)
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5736092Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5736288Z I will:
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5736536Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5736841Z  - make sure your computer doesn't already have Nix files
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5737372Z    (if it does, I will tell you how to clean them up.)
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5737854Z  - create local users (see the list above for the users I'll make)
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5738385Z  - create a local group (nixbld)
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5738789Z  - install Nix in /nix
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5739167Z  - create a configuration file in /etc/nix
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5739646Z  - set up the "default profile" by creating some Nix-related files in
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5740069Z    /root
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5749710Z  - back up /etc/bash.bashrc to /etc/bash.bashrc.backup-before-nix
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5751087Z  - update /etc/bash.bashrc to include some Nix configuration
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5759217Z  - load and start a service (at /etc/systemd/system/nix-daemon.service
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5759946Z    and /etc/systemd/system/nix-daemon.socket) for nix-daemon
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5760262Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5763849Z [32m[4;32mReady to continue?
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5764509Z [0mNo TTY, assuming you would say yes :)
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5778128Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5778919Z [34m---- let's talk about sudo -----------------------------------------------------
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5786832Z [0mThis script is going to call sudo a lot. Normally, it would show you
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5787779Z exactly what commands it is running and why. However, the script is
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5788287Z run in a headless fashion, like this:
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5788495Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5788804Z   $ curl -L https://nixos.org/nix/install | sh
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5789492Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5789777Z or maybe in a CI pipeline. Because of that, I'm going to skip the
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5790237Z verbose output in the interest of brevity.
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5790879Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5791031Z If you would like to
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5791361Z see the output, try like this:
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5791573Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5791860Z   $ curl -L -o install-nix https://nixos.org/nix/install
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5792328Z   $ sh ./install-nix
2026-11-07T06:33:15.5792521Z 
